<header class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Publishing handoff</p><h1>Take the article into your next tool.</h1><p class="lead">Choose a formatted clipboard copy, one article file, or a kit with the files and instructions together. Inspect the result in your own destination before publishing.</p></header>
<h2 id="publishing-profiles">Reuse your publishing routine</h2><p>Open Reuse a publishing setup below the destination selector in the writing workspace or any converter. Choose your destination and title options, name the setup, and optionally add up to eight personal reminders, one per line. The checklist appears beside export approval. Ticking a reminder never approves an export, and reminders do not block exporting. Changes to article fields, source notes, review questions, reminders, or export choices reset its ticks. The reminders are your own review routine, not automated verification.</p><p>Choose Download publishing profile to save the current choices and reminder text as a small JSON file. On your next visit, load that file in the same panel, inspect the destination, title choices, and checklist, then choose Apply profile. Applying changes export choices, starts reminders unchecked, and clears approval while keeping all current writing. Cancel preview leaves current choices and approval intact. A profile never connects to your website or publishes anything.</p><p>Profiles exclude article fields, source notes, audience, purpose, private review questions, completed ticks, and approval. Their name and reminder text are unencrypted, so keep private details out of a file you intend to share. Profiles have no autosave. Workspace backups keep writing in their existing format and do not include profiles. Restoring a workspace or checkpoint, loading a fictional sample, or clearing the desk removes the current profile name and checklist and returns exports to the route defaults. A normal article import keeps the setup and clears checklist ticks and approval. Remove profile name and checklist clears those fields while keeping the current export choices.</p>
<h2>Find the result without leaving your draft</h2><p>On the WordPress, HTML, and Word converter pages, wider screens put the editor beside the reading preview and selected handoff. On smaller screens, the article comes first, then its result. Long desktop previews scroll independently: focus the preview region and use the arrow or page keys to read through it. The preview format explanation is under Preview formats and limits.</p><p>Use More export options for formatted copying, body files, reading pages, page Markdown, and the general publishing kit. Choosing Another editor or Markdown site opens that group. The WordPress-specific kit stays in its own selected handoff. How to paste these blocks in WordPress opens the destination steps. Save, import, and revisions remain above the editor; source notes and draft checks remain available through the existing tools. Opening or closing a view does not edit or approve the article.</p>
<h2 id="word-document">Take an editable Word document</h2><p>Use the <a href="/markdown-to-word/">Markdown to Word converter</a>, or choose Word document (.docx) under Where will you take this article in the workspace. Read the preview and choose whether to include the article title. Only the body is required unless you select the title. After reviewing, download Word and inspect the actual file in your editor before sharing.</p><p>Headings, common emphasis, links, code, supported nested numbering, and simple header tables become document content. Source HTML, images in Markdown, and unsupported syntax remain text; no source image, macro, embedded file, comment, or tracked change is generated. Page layout and fonts can vary. The file omits page metadata, source notes, private review notes, preferences, and history. It is an unencrypted article export, separate from a private workspace backup. Direct .docx import is not supported; use an explicit formatted selection or UTF-8 HTML import to bring document writing into this tab.</p>
<h2 id="wordpress">Take editable blocks into WordPress</h2><p>Paste plain text or simple Markdown in <a href="/write/#draft">the writing workspace</a>, read the preview, and review your claims and permissions. Choose WordPress block editor under Choose your export, then check the review approval box. Page metadata is optional.</p><ol><li>Open a new, empty draft post on your own WordPress site. Put the title in its separate title field. Copy title separately is available when a valid title is present.</li><li>Select Copy WordPress blocks. In the WordPress editor, open Options, select Code editor, and paste into the post body. Use the full post Code editor, not a Custom HTML block or the Classic Editor. Start with an empty post to avoid replacing existing writing.</li><li>Return to Visual editor. The supported content becomes editable paragraph, heading, nested list, quote, code, table, and selected linked Image blocks. Check paragraph breaks, emphasis, links, and heading levels. Body H1 headings become H2, and numbered lists preserve their starting value.</li><li>Inspect selected linked images, add other images, and place excerpt, slug, and any SEO description in the appropriate WordPress fields or plugin. Page fields are not inserted into the body. Inspect converted simple pipe tables. Rebuild malformed or oversized tables or lists, embeds, reference links, and other unsupported formatting in WordPress; their source stays literal text.</li><li>Preview the actual post, including its mobile view, before publishing yourself. Themes, plugins, and editor versions can change the result. Exporting does not verify factual claims or URLs.</li></ol><p>If clipboard access is unavailable, Download WordPress blocks provides an HTML file. Open it in a text editor to copy the complete source, including block comments, into the post Code editor. Opening the file in a browser shows a reading view and can request selected linked image URLs. Use a text editor to inspect the source without loading those images. Inspect or manually copy the approved blocks offers a selectable source field after review. Editing or changing destinations clears approval and that temporary output.</p><p>No WordPress credentials, API requests, image downloads, or website connection are involved. The title and page fields remain separate. Source notes and private review questions are excluded from the blocks. <a href="https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/wordpress-block-editor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official WordPress editor help (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2 id="article-links">Prepare article links</h2><p>Use Add or edit link below the article to add or correct inline web links without typing Markdown. Inspect the complete link reference before applying, or preview removal while keeping its original text. Article links lists recognized labels and URLs as text, without visiting them; its downloadable notes exclude private source and review fields. Changes save an in-tab checkpoint and clear approval. <a href="/help/local-workspace/#link-editor">Link editing steps and limits</a>.</p>
<h2 id="image-editor">Add images without writing Markdown</h2><p>Below the article body, choose Add or edit image. Select an omitted Word image placeholder by line number, use the current article selection, or place a new image at the article end. Ordinary paragraph text can be replaced; formatted phrases must be selected in full. Choose a blank line outside headings, lists, quotes, tables, source HTML, and code for other placements. Surrounding text is preserved exactly, with paragraph breaks added around the image.</p><p>Enter an existing public HTTPS image URL and a single line of alternative text. To deliberately use empty alternative text, clear the field and select This image is decorative. URLs are limited to 2,048 characters and alternative text to 500; oversized pastes are rejected in full. No image is fetched, uploaded, or checked. The editor escapes Markdown punctuation in alternative text and preserves the URL you enter. Unsupported URLs are rejected rather than repaired.</p><p>Select Preview image change to inspect the exact selected text and proposed Markdown, then Apply image change. Applying saves previous writing as an in-tab checkpoint and clears approval and the linked-image choice. Article edits invalidate the pending location and clear its preview; select a current location before trying again. Closing the panel clears its preview but keeps preparation. Discard image preparation keeps the article and approval. Unsaved image details are excluded from backups, checkpoints, collections, and exports. Apply first to keep them as article text. Replacing or clearing the workspace discards preparation.</p><p>For WordPress, explicitly select supported linked images in Images for WordPress after applying. Other exports and the local reading preview retain the image Markdown as literal text. In WordPress, inspect every image and use Upload to Media Library if you want a local copy on your site.</p>
<h2 id="wordpress-images">Prepare linked images for WordPress</h2><p>In the WordPress handoff, open Images for WordPress. The list shows recognized image references, their current alternative text, and whether they can be converted. Select Include supported linked images in WordPress blocks, review again, and copy or download. The same option is used by the complete WordPress kit. It defaults off, resets after body edits, imports/restores, leaving the WordPress destination, and applying or clearing a publishing profile, and is excluded from workspace and profile files.</p><p>A simple standalone image such as <code>![Participants at a fictional workshop](https://example.com/workshop.jpg)</code> must be the only content in its own paragraph. Only absolute HTTPS URLs without credentials are supported. Add a blank line before and after an image when it is beside other prose. Inline images among text, images inside lists, quotes, headings, tables or links, four-space/tab indentation, quoted titles, reference images, source HTML, and unsupported URLs stay literal. Images in code are not inspected or converted. No URL is repaired automatically.</p><p>Draft to Page never fetches or displays these image files. The list and Download image handoff notes contain text only, with the first 40 references shown on screen and all recognized references in the text file. Every eligible reference is converted when selected. Opening WordPress blocks in WordPress or a browser can request their image URLs. The image choice is separate from export approval and changing it clears approval. Opening the image panel or selecting an image to edit keeps writing and approval unchanged.</p><p>These are references to existing files, not Media Library uploads. Use public files you have permission to publish. In WordPress, inspect every image, edit alternative text or deliberately mark decorative images, and use Upload to Media Library if you want a copy stored on your site. Availability, file type, content, and permissions are unverified. No attachment ID, dimensions, caption, crop, or featured image is invented. <a href="https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/image-block/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official WordPress image instructions (new tab)</a>.</p><p>The local reading preview, reading-page file, HTML source, formatted copies, and Word documents continue to show image syntax as text. The original Markdown preserves its references. They are not visual previews of the linked images. In the WordPress kit, REVIEW.txt includes the current image mode and every recognized image reference, while README.txt explains when URLs can load and how to keep files in your own Media Library. Image handoff notes exclude private source/review fields, but image URLs or alt text you wrote can still be private; inspect before sharing.</p>
<h2 id="wordpress-kit">Keep the complete WordPress handoff together</h2><p>The workspace shows line-specific WordPress handoff findings while you edit. These combine limited writing-pattern checks with some format checks for literal images, source HTML, malformed or oversized tables or lists, reference links, task-list markers, strike-through, separators, body H1 headings, and unsupported list structure. Select Inspect line to focus and select the current passage. Nothing changes automatically. Edits refresh findings; switching destinations clears their hidden list. The first 40 findings are shown, with a count when more exist. This is not an exhaustive Markdown parser, quality score, or verification of facts, permissions, grammar, or working URLs.</p><p>Under Post fields &amp; complete WordPress kit, use Edit optional post fields to open the existing excerpt, filename, and description fields. Editing clears approval. After reviewing again, copy each field separately. Excerpt goes in the post Excerpt setting; filename becomes a suggested Slug/permalink when available; description belongs in your SEO tool only if it has that field. An excerpt is optional and its display depends on the theme. Blocks do not require these fields.</p><p>A WordPress kit needs a valid title and article. Empty optional fields remain empty. If the filename is empty or invalid, the kit uses a safe suggestion from the title and discloses it; the editor field is unchanged. Review the six-file list, approve, and choose Download WordPress kit. Extract the ZIP locally before using it.</p><table class="format-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">How to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">wordpress-blocks.html</th><td>Open in a text editor and copy all source, including block comments, into the full WordPress Code editor. Return to Visual editor and inspect.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">article-body.md</th><td>Keep the original Markdown body for further editing. It is not the block source.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">reading-page.html</th><td>Open in a browser to review the article. It does not predict every theme or plugin.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">page-details.json</th><td>Manually place title and optional excerpt, slug, and description in their appropriate fields. This is not a CMS import file.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">REVIEW.txt</th><td>Current line-specific findings, all recognized image references and their selected mode, and a final publishing checklist. Regenerate the kit after article edits.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">README.txt</th><td>Follow the WordPress-specific steps and field mapping.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The kit excludes private source and review notes, revisions, and authentication. Its article and page fields can still contain private information you entered. It is unencrypted, bounded to 4 MiB, does not restore a workspace, and does not upload or publish. Save a separate private workspace backup for unfinished work. <a href="https://wordpress.org/documentation/article/page-post-settings-sidebar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official WordPress field help (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2>Paste into a website editor</h2><ol><li><a href="/write/">Finish your article in the workspace</a>, compare it with your source, and check the review box.</li><li>Select Copy formatted article. Basic headings, lists, emphasis, code, and supported web links are included. The default copy excludes the title, so you can enter it in your editor's separate title field.</li><li>If your destination needs a document title inside the body, select Include the title when copying formatted text and review again before copying.</li><li>Paste into an editor that accepts HTML clipboard content. The copy also includes a plain-text representation with readable link addresses.</li><li>Check the actual pasted headings, lists, paragraph breaks, title, links, and mobile preview before publishing.</li></ol><p>Browser and destination support varies. Some editors discard formatting or change it. This is a clipboard handoff, not a verified CMS integration. If formatted copying is unavailable or blocked, Copy article body supplies Markdown, Download reading page provides HTML, and the visible preview can be selected manually. Your writing stays in the workspace.</p>
<h2>Download a publishing kit</h2><p>Enter a valid title and article, approve the result, and expand Take a complete publishing kit. Inspect the file list and notices, then select Download publishing kit. The ZIP is prepared locally. Extract it into a local folder and open its reading page first.</p>
<table class="format-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What to do with it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">article-body.md</th><td>Continue editing or paste into a destination that accepts Markdown. Title and page fields are excluded.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">reading-page.html</th><td>Open locally to read and inspect the article. Manually add it to static hosting only after reviewing its destination name and preview.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">page-details.json</th><td>Use its title, excerpt, description, and suggested safe filename for a manual handoff. Empty fields stay empty. This is not an article import or a universal CMS import file.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">page.md, when included</th><td>Use complete page Markdown in a repository that supports its metadata. The format choice determines whether the file contains a title heading.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">README.txt</th><td>Follow the file-specific next steps and destination review checklist.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Finish page details when needed</h2><p>A kit needs only the title and article. Complete the excerpt, description, and a valid filename to include page.md. Otherwise the preview and README list the fields to finish. For page.md, expand Markdown with page details and choose Website supplies the title heading or Include a title heading in the file.</p><p>If the filename is empty or invalid, the ZIP filename and page-details slug use a safe suggestion from the title. The original editor field is kept, and the preview explains this decision. The kit does not invent missing summaries, dates, layout settings, or facts.</p>
<h2>Choose one destination format</h2><p>Do not upload the whole kit as a website. For a Markdown repository, add page.md on a feature branch and adapt its metadata to the actual site. For static HTML, choose a destination name for reading-page.html in a directory that serves HTML. For another editor, use the clipboard or article body and enter page fields manually. <a href="/help/markdown-export/">Review the export guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Keep article files and private work separate</h2><p>The kit and clipboard copies exclude source notes, audience, purpose, article type, private review questions, check results, and sign-in fields. They can still contain private information you put in the article. Review that content before sharing or adding files to a public repository.</p><p>A publishing kit is unencrypted and cannot restore a complete workspace. Use an explicit <a href="/help/local-workspace/">private workspace backup</a> separately to keep source and review notes. Export preferences and temporary kit previews are excluded from those version-1 backups.</p>
<h2 id="html-source">Take clean HTML source</h2><p>For a website or editor that accepts HTML code, choose HTML source for a website in the workspace, or open the <a href="/markdown-to-html/">free Markdown to HTML converter</a>. Read the preview, choose whether to include the title as an H1 heading, and check the review box. Then use Copy HTML source or Download HTML fragment. Body-only conversion needs no title or page metadata. Changing title handling or any writing clears approval.</p><p>The output is an article fragment, without a document head, page metadata, scripts, external assets, or WordPress block comments. Open the downloaded fragment in a text editor to copy the code, or inspect and manually copy the approved source in the workspace. Use an HTML source field in your destination; ordinary text editors can display tags literally. Copy formatted article is the separate choice for visible rich-text pasting. Download reading page creates a complete standalone document instead.</p><p>The fragment keeps supported headings, lists, tables, emphasis, quotes, code, and safe web links. Explicit line breaks and fixed inline table alignment and wrapping hints avoid depending entirely on this site's stylesheet. Your destination can strip styles or render differently, so preview there. Source HTML and images stay literal text, and private source notes, review questions, and revisions are excluded. The title is omitted unless selected; body H1 headings become H2. No upload or publication occurs.</p>
<h2>Know what the handoff covers</h2><p>The reading renderer and formatted copy share the same limited Markdown subset. Source HTML, images, relative URLs, and unsupported links remain text. Supported absolute web links load only when a reader follows them. The ZIP uses ordinary stored text files without compression, under a 4 MiB bound. Files carry a fixed archive timestamp, not an edit-history timestamp.</p><p>Exporting does not upload, connect to a repository, approve claims, or publish. The draft flag is metadata and works only when your destination honors it. Check the actual saved files, rendering, links, permissions, and factual claims in the destination.</p><p><a class="button" href="/write/#exports">Open review &amp; export</a> · <a href="/guides/review/">Use the review checklist</a></p>

<h2>Bring a Markdown table</h2><p>Simple pipe tables become editable WordPress Table blocks, formatted tables, and tables in reading files. A header row and delimiter row define up to 12 columns. Every data row needs a pipe and the same number of cells as the header, with at most 100 data rows per table. Use at least three dashes per delimiter cell; colons set column alignment. Escape a literal pipe as \|, including inside inline code. Mismatched or oversized tables stay complete as text and receive a handoff finding. Merged cells, multiline cells, nested tables, and source HTML tables are outside this subset. Inspect your destination layout and add a caption there if needed.</p>

<h2>Keep lists and subpoints together</h2><p>Bullets using -, *, or + and numbered markers ending in . or ) become editable lists. Their first number is preserved, including a checklist continuing at 4. Nest single-line items using two or four more spaces per level. Use at most six levels and 200 items per continuous group; blank lines start a new group. Ordered markers must be consecutive or all 1. Irregular indentation, inconsistent numbers, empty items, excess size, tab indentation, and multiline blocks reject the whole candidate group without discarding source. The handoff finding selects its current first line. Task checkboxes remain literal text and need destination work.</p>
